        "title": "The Link Layer - Ethernet, MAC, Framing, Switching, VLANs, ARP",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The link layer (L2) has exactly one job: **move a frame across one physical\nsegment, from one network interface to the next, on a single local network.** It\nknows nothing about the Internet, nothing about routing, nothing about your\napplication. Its entire universe is \"this LAN.\" Everything bigger than the local\nsegment is L3's problem (02). What L2 gives you is the foundational primitive:\n*addressable delivery between NICs that share a wire (or a switch).*"
    },
    {
      "id": "01-LINK-LAYER.md#003",
      "path": [
        "01-LINK-LAYER.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:networking:01-link-layer, git-history:networking:01-link-layer]",
        "canonical_path": "networking/01-LINK-LAYER.md",
        "concepts": "[ethernet, mac address, framing, switching, vlan, arp, spanning tree]",
        "current_path": "networking/01-LINK-LAYER.md",
        "id": "maxim:networking:link-layer",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "networking",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[link layer]",
        "section": "networking",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "The Link Layer - Ethernet, MAC, Framing, Switching, VLANs, ARP",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n                        ONE BROADCAST DOMAIN (one LAN / one VLAN)\n   +-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   |                                                                   |\n   |   HOST A          HOST B          HOST C          ROUTER          |\n   |  +------+        +------+        +------+        +--------+        |\n   |  | NIC  |        | NIC  |        | NIC  |        | NIC    |        |\n   |  | MAC  |        | MAC  |        | MAC  |        | (gw)   |        |\n   |  | ..a1 |        | ..b2 |        | ..c3 |        | ..ff   |        |\n   |  +--+---+        +--+---+        +--+---+        +---+----+        |\n   |     |               |               |               |            |\n   |     +-------+-------+-------+-------+-------+-------+              |\n   |             |       |       |       |       |                     |\n   |          +--+-------+-------+-------+-------+--+                   |\n   |          |          ETHERNET SWITCH         |                    |\n   |          |  (forwards frames by MAC table)  |                    |\n   |          +----------------------------------+                    |\n   |                                                                   |\n   +-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n       Frames carry MAC src/dst. The switch reads dst MAC, looks it up,\n       and sends the frame out exactly the right port. The ROUTER is the\n       only way OUT of this broadcast domain (to other LANs / the Internet).\n```"
    },
